## DESCRIPTION
## Find the matrix representing a map.
## ENDDESCRIPTION


## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Linear transformations)
## DBsection(Associated matrices)
## Date(September 5, 2008)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## KEYWORDS('homomorphism', 'matrix representation')

# updated 2023.11.02

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl',);

Context('Vector');

do {
    ($a1, $a2, $a3) = (random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3));
    ($b1, $b2, $b3) = (random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3));
} until abs($a1 * $b2 - $a2 * $b1) + abs($a2 * $b3 - $a3 * $b2) != 0;

$ans = Matrix([ [ $a1, $b1 ], [ $a2, $b2 ], [ $a3, $b3 ] ]);

BEGIN_PGML

Consider the homomorphism [`h: \mathbb{R}^2 \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^3`] such that
[```
\left[ \begin{array}{r} 1 \\ 0 \end{array} \right] \mapsto
\left[ \begin{array}{r} [$a1] \\ [$a2] \\ [$a3]  \end{array} \right] \qquad
\left[ \begin{array}{r} 0 \\ 1 \end{array} \right] \mapsto
\left[ \begin{array}{r} [$b1] \\ [$b2] \\ [$b3]  \end{array} \right]
```]

Use the standard basis to find the matrix representing this map.

[___]*{$ans}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
In this case, because the standard basis are the input vectors for [`h`], then the
representation matrix is the two column vectors or 

[```[$ans]```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();    # This should be the last executable line in the problem.
